/**
\page org_mitk_views_mxnlayouteditor The MxN Layout Editor

\imageMacro{QmitkMxNMultiWidgetEditor.svg,"Icon of the MxN Layout Editor",16.00}

\tableofcontents

\section org_mitk_views_mxnlayouteditor_Overview Overview
	The MxN Layout Editor is the authoritative view of the whole layout document of an
	MxN display (\ref org_mitk_editors_mxnmultiwidget): the window arrangement and all
	synchronization groups. Open it by clicking the sync barcode in any window's utility
	menu, with "Open layout editor" from a window view's context menu, or via
	<i>Window &rarr; Show View</i>.
	As a dockable view it stays open during review sessions and doubles as a live
	legend of the current coupling.

	The windows themselves are arranged where they are, in the display: while the view is
	visible, every window shows its sync plate, and the plates are where windows are
	selected and assigned (see \ref org_mitk_views_mxnlayouteditor_Arrange). In the view,
	the synchronization side has two faces on their own tabs: <b>Sync groups</b>, the
	group cards used for everyday work, and <b>Advanced</b>, the per-window link matrix.
	Only one is up at a time.

	\imageMacroEx{QmitkMxN_LayoutEditor.png, "The MxN Layout Editor view", 8, 360px}

\section org_mitk_views_mxnlayouteditor_Layout The layout row
	The <b>Layout</b> row at the top of the view acts on the layout document as a whole:
	<ul>
		<li> <b>Presets</b> applies one of the arrangements that ship with MITK.
		<li> <b>Load...</b> replaces the current layout with one read from a layout file
			(see \ref org_mitk_editors_mxnmultiwidget_LayoutFile).
		<li> <b>Save...</b> writes the current layout to a file. A saved layout carries the
			synchronization groups as well as the window arrangement, so this is also how
			a change made purely on the synchronization side is kept.
	</ul>

	Applying a preset or a file replaces the window arrangement and discards the
	current synchronization groups, so the view asks for confirmation first unless the
	layout is still the trivial default.

	The grid of the arrangement is edited in the row below: the row and column buttons
	add or remove a trailing row or column in place, which leaves the existing windows
	and their settings untouched, while <b>Edit grid...</b> opens the picker for a grid of
	a chosen size or for an arrangement derived from the loaded data.

\section org_mitk_views_mxnlayouteditor_Arrange Arranging the windows
	While the MxN Layout Editor is visible, its MxN display is in <i>arrange mode</i>:
	every window that has room for one shows its sync plate - the same plate that pointing
	at a window's sync barcode brings up for a moment (see
	\ref org_mitk_editors_mxnmultiwidget_Utility) - and keeps it up. Closing the view,
	hiding it behind another view, or clicking the close button on a plate ends arrange
	mode and takes the plates away. Each plate shows the window's eight synchronization
	axes in the fixed order pan, zoom, slice, crosshair, orientation, windowing, LUT and
	data selection, tinted with the hue of the group the window is linked to, and the
	window's name.

	\imageMacroEx{QmitkMxN_ArrangeMode.png, "Arrange mode: the plates while the view is open with two windows selected and the pointer on the zoom axis", 16, 800px}

	Nothing else about the display changes. Only the plates themselves take the mouse:
	scrolling, the crosshair, zoom and pan work everywhere else in the window exactly as
	before, so a synchronization can be tried out the moment it is made.

	Pointing at a glyph on a plate enlarges that axis on every plate, names it above the
	row, and draws the frame of every window that shares this window's synchronization on
	that axis bold in the group's hue - which windows belong together is visible at a
	glance. The thin frames keep showing each window's group as always; only the bold ones
	answer the axis you point at. Pointing at a glyph on a group card, a chip in the
	<b>Advanced</b> matrix or a window's sync barcode does the same; the bold frames always
	answer the last thing pointed at. A group card dragged over a window marks that
	window's frame as the drop target.

\subsection org_mitk_views_mxnlayouteditor_Selection Selecting windows
	Selection follows the file-explorer model: a click on a plate selects that window,
	Ctrl-click toggles a single window, and Shift-click takes every window in the rectangle
	spanned from the window clicked last (Ctrl+Shift adds that range to the selection).
	Clicking the only selected window again deselects it, and "Clear selection" in the
	plate menu selects nothing. A selected window's plate is framed in the selection color.
	The first selected window also becomes the active one in the display, and clicking a
	window's image selects that window.

\subsection org_mitk_views_mxnlayouteditor_Assign Assigning windows to a group
	Selected windows join a group by dragging their plates onto the group card, by
	dragging the card onto a window, with "Add to group" from the plate menu, or with "Add
	selected windows" from the card's "..." menu. A card may be dropped anywhere on a
	window, not only on its plate. Dropped on a selected window, it takes the whole
	selection; dropped on any other, just that window. "Remove from group" in the plate
	menu, or "Remove selected windows" in the card's menu, takes windows out again; the
	plate menu lists every group the window is on, because a window can be linked to
	different groups on different axes.

	A drop can join in three ways. A plain drop <b>replaces</b>: the window leaves its
	other groups and wholly joins the target. Holding <i>Alt</i> <b>merges</b>, keeping
	the window's links on dimensions the target group does not cover. Holding
	<i>Shift</i> <b>fills</b> only the dimensions on which the window is not yet
	synchronized. Dragging with the right mouse button instead asks for the mode in a
	menu when the drop happens. The plate menu's "Add to group" replaces, like a plain
	drop.

	A window that joins a group which does not synchronize anything yet gets the
	navigation bundle (pan, zoom, slice, crosshair) as the common-case default.

\subsection org_mitk_views_mxnlayouteditor_PlateButtons The plate buttons
	The plate under the pointer carries two buttons in its top-right corner: the menu
	button (three dots) opens the plate menu, which a right-click on the plate opens as
	well, and the close button ends arrange mode.

\subsection org_mitk_views_mxnlayouteditor_ArrangeLimits Limits
	<ul>
		<li> A window too small to hold a legible plate shows none, so it cannot be clicked
			to select it. A group card can still be dropped onto it, and the <b>Advanced</b>
			matrix reaches every window.
		<li> While one window is maximized, only its plate is shown; the hidden windows can
			be arranged once the grid is restored. The view warns about it while it is the
			case.
		<li> Clean view hides the rest of the window furniture, but not the plates and
			the bold frames while the view is open.
		<li> A plate covers the middle of its window. The image under it stays live, but
			mouse interaction that starts on the plate goes to the plate.
	</ul>

\section org_mitk_views_mxnlayouteditor_Groups Group cards
	Each group has a card on the <b>Sync groups</b> tab; <b>+ Group</b> creates a new one.
	A card is one object: it can be dragged onto a window, and windows can be dropped onto it
	(see \ref org_mitk_views_mxnlayouteditor_Assign).
	<ul>
		<li> The header bar is drawn in the group's hue - the color that identifies the group
			on every synchronization surface - and carries the group's display name and how
			many windows it has.
		<li> The axis strip below shows which of the eight axes the group synchronizes.
			Clicking an axis links or unlinks it for every window in the group; an axis
			drawn dashed is linked for only some of them, and one with a filled upper
			corner has at least one member sitting at an offset - the card speaks for
			several windows, so it marks that a shift exists without saying whose. For
			a group that has no windows yet, clicking an axis only prepares which
			dimensions the first windows assigned to it will get.
	</ul>
	The "..." button in the header opens the less frequent actions:
	<ul>
		<li> "Rename..." and "Change color..." set the display name and the hue. Both are
			saved with the layout.
		<li> "Add selected windows" and "Remove selected windows" act on the windows
			selected on the plates.
		<li> "Link navigation" links the pan, zoom, slice and crosshair bundle for every
			member at once.
		<li> "Re-converge" re-establishes reference + offset for the group's
			slice/zoom/pan members after drift (e.g. from boundary clamping or zooming).
		<li> "Fit views to visible data" frames the group's slice/orientation
			neighborhood around the data visible in its first window; only these windows
			are affected, unlike the application-wide "Fit views to ..." actions.
		<li> "Delete group" unsynchronizes the group's windows and removes the group, after
			asking when it still has windows. The default group "main" cannot be deleted,
			because it is where every window's data selection falls back to.
	</ul>

\section org_mitk_views_mxnlayouteditor_Advanced The advanced matrix
	The <b>Advanced</b> tab is the power-user face: every window as a row, every
	dimension as a column. Each cell shows the group the window links on that dimension
	as a chip in the group's color, carrying the group's name and, where one was
	authored, the offset. An empty cell is a window that is not synchronized on that
	dimension. The data-selection column is never empty, because a window's selection
	always belongs to some group. A column widens to keep its chips whole, so names
	are only ever shortened by a width you set yourself - widen the view rather than
	read an abbreviation.

	This face exposes the complete layout-file link model, including couplings between
	windows that are not adjacent on screen, and it is the deliberate way to author a
	partial group state in which only some windows are linked on a dimension. Everyday
	assignment is expected to happen on the plates instead.

\subsection org_mitk_views_mxnlayouteditor_AdvancedEditing Editing the matrix
	The matrix is read; the bar beneath it is where it is edited, so nothing ever covers
	the grid being worked on. Select cells - drag across the grid, <i>Ctrl</i>-click to
	add one, <i>Shift</i>-click for a range - and the bar names the selection and offers
	what applies to it.

	When the selection is on a single dimension the bar carries that axis's glyph beside
	its name, the same glyph the column header and the plates use; a selection spanning
	several dimensions shows none, because no one glyph would name it.

	Selecting windows on their plates selects their rows here, and selecting cells here
	selects those windows on the plates. Hovering a chip marks every window sharing that
	synchronization, here and in the display at once. Clicking an axis header takes that
	whole column, clicking a window header that whole row, so linking every window on
	one dimension, or unlinking one window entirely, is two gestures.

	<ul>
		<li> <b>Group</b> assigns every selected cell. The list holds the groups that
			exist; groups are created with <b>+ Group</b>, in the same corner as on the
			<b>Sync groups</b> tab. Choosing "(not linked)" unlinks the selection, and
			on the data-selection axis returns the windows to the default group. The
			same list opens as a popup on right-click or double-click for a single
			quick edit, and <i>Del</i> unlinks the selection.
		<li> <b>Offset</b> appears for a selection that sits wholly on slice, zoom or pan
			and whose cells are all linked - an offset is relative to the group's seed,
			so an unlinked cell has nothing for it to be relative to. This is how a
			detail view is kept twice as zoomed, or a window parked a fixed distance
			from its group. Where the selected cells carry different offsets the editor
			reads "multiple" rather than any one of them, and that marker cannot be
			applied: dial a real value to give the whole selection one.
		<li> <b>Ramp</b> spreads slice offsets over the selected cells in the order the
			windows are listed in the line above, which authors a movie-frame layout
			showing slices -1 / 0 / +1 in one step. The offsets it would write are
			previewed beside it, so the effect reads against that window list before it
			is applied. It needs a selection wholly in one group: offsets in different
			groups are measured from different seeds, so a series spread across them
			would mean nothing. Slice only, for the same reason in a different form -
			zoom composes multiplicatively rather than by steps, and a pan ramp has no
			unambiguous direction in two dimensions.
	</ul>

	A change applies as soon as it is made and both faces show it at once, so an
	assignment made on the cards is on the chips here without reopening the tab.

*/
